<section id="research" class="paper-section">
  <div class="container paper-shell">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-kicker">Research</span>
      <h2>Formalizing federated PKI as a governed weighted graph.</h2>
      <p>
        The proposal focuses on controlled interoperability between multidomain PKIs,
        representing certification authorities and federation links as a directed,
        weighted graph that can be queried for valid and optimal certification paths.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="research-grid">
      <article class="research-card">
        <div class="research-card-header">
          <h3>Multidomain PKI complexity</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          Traditional cross-certification and mesh-like trust structures become hard to
          govern as domains, policies, and interoperability constraints increase.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>RFC 4158 highlights the intrinsic difficulty of path building in dense trust topologies.</li>
          <li>Bridge CAs and trust lists reduce friction, but not always deterministic route selection.</li>
          <li>The model targets explicit governance of inter-domain trust relationships.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="research-card research-card-figure">
        <div class="mini-chart" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="mini-chart-toolbar">
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
            <span></span>
          </div>
